   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "The graph of the continuous function h shown consists of four line segments and a semicircle, for \\( 0 \\le t \\le 12 \\). Let \\( g(x) = \\int_{4}^{2x} h(t)\\,dt \\). Find \\( g'(\\frac{5}{2}) \\).",
      "command_verb": "find"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": -2
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus and the chain rule, \\( g'(x) = h(2x) \\cdot 2 \\), since the upper limit 2x has derivative 2.",
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99024",
         "rule_named": "Fundamental Theorem of Calculus with a composite upper limit"
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "At x = 5/2 the upper limit is t = 5, the midpoint of the segment from (4, 0) to (6, -2), so \\( h(5) = -1 \\).",
         "mathjson": -1,
         "rule_named": "value read from the graph"
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "So \\( g'(\\frac{5}{2}) = 2 \\cdot \\left(-1\\right) = -2 \\).",
         "mathjson": -2,
         "rule_named": "chain rule factor"
      }
   ],
